# Rank 1012 / CVE-2023-43800: Arduino Create Agent privilege escalation through
# the /v2/pkgs/tools/installed endpoint. Tools.Install accepted a caller-supplied
# URL and installed from it, and the only integrity control was a checksum taken
# from the same request payload:
#
#   if payload.URL != nil {
#       return c.install(ctx, path, *payload.URL, *payload.Checksum)
#   }
#
# install() downloads the URL, extracts the archive into the tools folder, and
# only then compares its SHA-256 against the supplied checksum. Because the
# attacker supplies both the URL and the checksum, the comparison proves the
# bytes arrived intact but proves nothing about who produced them, so anyone who
# can reach the localhost endpoint installs an arbitrary archive and runs code as
# the agent user (CWE-345). The pool commit was a later HEAD-handler feature;
# corrected to the real fix 33080c3, which requires a signature and verifies it
# against a trusted RSA public key before installing:
# utilities.VerifyInput(*payload.URL, *payload.Signature).
#
# ATTENTION, vocabulary and expressiveness gap.
#  - No concept covers this weakness class. The closest,
#    code.NodePreGypRemoteBinaryInstall (CWE-494) and
#    code.InsecureExecutableDownload (CWE-319), describe a package manifest
#    fetching a remote binary and a cleartext download; neither is "the artifact
#    URL and its integrity value arrive in the same untrusted message".
#  - The concept that belongs here is general and worth building: an integrity
#    value supplied by the same untrusted party as the artifact it certifies
#    provides no authenticity, and the control is a signature checked against a
#    trusted key. Expressing it needs a provenance comparison between two values
#    (the URL and the checksum both originate from one request payload), which
#    is a dataflow relation the presence-node binding layer cannot state. A
#    binding keyed on the literal selectors payload.URL and payload.Checksum
#    would be repro-shaped: an updater using req.DownloadURL and req.SHA256
#    walks straight past it, so it fails the genericity test.
#  - The available taint framing is also wrong and would not discriminate the
#    fix. Labelling the request payload as a source makes the URL reach the
#    code.UrlFetch sink already detected at tools.go:181 and reports SSRF
#    (CWE-918), but the fix still fetches the same caller-supplied URL and only
#    adds a signature check, so that finding would fire on the vulnerable and
#    fixed revisions alike.
#
# verify_current_expected: attention-vocabulary-gap

test "rank1012: tool install trusts a URL and checksum from the same request payload"
  lang go
  file v2/pkgs/tools.go
  reject VYQL-RF-001
  code
  ```
  package pkgs

  import (
      "context"
      "path/filepath"

      "github.com/arduino/arduino-create-agent/gen/tools"
  )

  func (c *Tools) Install(ctx context.Context, payload *tools.ToolPayload) (*tools.Operation, error) {
      path := filepath.Join(payload.Packager, payload.Name, payload.Version)

      if payload.URL != nil {
          return c.install(ctx, path, *payload.URL, *payload.Checksum)
      }

      return nil, tools.MakeNotFound(nil)
  }
  ```

test "rank1012: fix verifies an RSA signature over the URL before installing"
  lang go
  file v2/pkgs/tools.go
  reject VYQL-RF-001
  code
  ```
  package pkgs

  import (
      "context"
      "path/filepath"

      "github.com/arduino/arduino-create-agent/gen/tools"
      "github.com/arduino/arduino-create-agent/utilities"
  )

  func (c *Tools) Install(ctx context.Context, payload *tools.ToolPayload) (*tools.Operation, error) {
      path := filepath.Join(payload.Packager, payload.Name, payload.Version)

      if payload.URL != nil && payload.Signature != nil && payload.Checksum != nil {
          err := utilities.VerifyInput(*payload.URL, *payload.Signature)
          if err != nil {
              return nil, err
          }
          return c.install(ctx, path, *payload.URL, *payload.Checksum)
      }

      return nil, tools.MakeNotFound(nil)
  }
  ```
